==English==

===Etymology===
Shortening of {{suffix|en|away|ward}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈweɪwə(ɹ)d/}}, {{enPR|wā'wərd}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-wayward.wav|a=Southern England}}
* {{rhymes|en|eɪwə(ɹ)d|s=2}}

===Adjective===
{{en-adj}}

# Given to a [[wilful]], [[perverse]] [[deviation]] from the [[expected]] [[norm]]; tending to [[stray]].
#* {{RQ:Doyle Return of Sherlock Holmes|chapter=The Adventure of the Three Students|passage=He is a brilliant fellow when he chooses to work—one of the brightest intellects of the University, but he is '''wayward''', dissipated, and unprincipled.}}
#* {{RQ:Montgomery Anne of Avonlea|page=66|passage=Fancies are like shadows .&nbsp;.&nbsp;. you can’t cage them, they’re such '''wayward''', dancing things.}}
# [[obstinate|Obstinate]], [[contrary]] and [[unpredictable]].
#: {{syn|en|Thesaurus:obstinate}}
# {{lb|en|sports}} Not on target.
#* {{quote-journal
|en
|date=September 2, 2011
|author=Phil McNulty
|title=Bulgaria 0-3 England
|work=BBC
|url=http://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/14770875.stm
